  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 4,
    "lesson_title": "The Complex Variable $s=\\sigma+j\\omega$",
    "lesson_core_move": "Convert an exponentially weighted Fourier integral into Laplace-domain notation by identifying $s=\\sigma+j\\omega$, with $\\sigma$ setting the convergence-producing exponential weight and $\\omega$ setting the sinusoidal frequency.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "The Complex Variable $s=\\sigma+j\\omega$",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to The Complex Variable $s=\\sigma+j\\omega$",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "When you try to use a Fourier kernel and the integral $\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}x(t)e^{-j\\omega t}\\,dt$ fails to converge, the previous lesson shows that adding an exponential weight is the remedy. You now package that idea so students can reuse it later in Laplace notation.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The direct action is to replace separate factors with a single combined representation. Start from $e^{-\\sigma t}$ and $e^{-j\\omega t}$ and write them using one compact symbol $s$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Define the complex variable as $s=\\sigma+j\\omega$. In this definition, $\\sigma$ is the real part that controls exponential weighting, and $\\omega$ is the angular-frequency part that controls oscillation.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "s=\\sigma+j\\omega",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply the rule to combine the product of factors into one exponential: $e^{-\\sigma t}e^{-j\\omega t}=e^{-st}$. This keeps later derivations short and consistent.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{-\\sigma t}e^{-j\\omega t}=e^{-(\\sigma+j\\omega)t}=e^{-st}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "s\\big|_{\\sigma=0}=j\\omega",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "The Complex Variable $s=\\sigma+j\\omega$",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Identifying $\\sigma$ and $\\omega$ in $s=\\sigma+j\\omega$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Write $s=-2+j\\,7$ as \"$\\sigma$ is ... and $\\omega$ is ...\"",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the target representation from this lesson, $s=\\sigma+j\\omega$, as the cue. You should match each part of the given number to that template without rearranging terms.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The rule is: the part not multiplied by $j$ is the real part, so it is $\\sigma$; the coefficient of $j$ is the imaginary part, so it is $\\omega$. This is the same extraction you will reuse in every later section.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "s=-2+j\\,7=(-2)+j(7) \\Rightarrow \\sigma=-2,\\;\\omega=7",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Therefore, the answer is \"$\\sigma$ is $-2$ and $\\omega$ is $7$\". A negative $\\sigma$ means the real part is negative in $s=\\sigma+j\\omega$, so the associated exponential factor in $e^{-st}$ is growing in time if used directly.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "s=\\sigma+j\\,\\omega \\text{ with } \\omega=0 \\Rightarrow s=\\sigma",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So for the edge case $\\omega=0$, the same rule gives zero oscillation and $s$ is purely real; that is the special $j\\omega$-axis condition you will use in the next practice model.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "The Complex Variable $s=\\sigma+j\\omega$",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Combining $e^{-\\sigma t}$ and $e^{-j\\omega t}$ into $e^{-st}$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Show that $e^{-2t}\\,e^{-j4t}=e^{-st}$ and find $s$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Keep the same recognition target from the previous steps: the final form is $e^{-st}$. Here, the cue is already set by one factor with real exponent and one with imaginary exponent, both in the same variable $t$ and both with a leading minus sign.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply the composition rule $e^A e^B=e^{A+B}$ with $A=-2t$ and $B=-j4t$, then merge them into one exponent.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{-2t}e^{-j4t}=e^{-2t-j4t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now factor $t$ and write the combined exponent in canonical form $-(\\sigma+j\\omega)t$. Since $-2t-j4t=-(2+j4)t$, we identify $\\sigma=2$ and $\\omega=4$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{-2t-j4t}=e^{-(2+j4)t}=e^{-st},\\; s=2+j4",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The result is the same rule in reverse of the previous step: this time you reconstruct the single $s$ from two factors, so the answer is that the expression is in $e^{-st}$ form with $s=2+j4$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "The Complex Variable $s=\\sigma+j\\omega$",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Rewriting an Exponentially Weighted Fourier Integral in Terms of $s$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Let $X(\\sigma,\\omega)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}x(t)e^{-3t}e^{-j\\omega t}\\,dt$. Rewrite this as $X(s)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This example is the weighted-integral form of the same rule: the cue is a product of two exponentials inside the transform integral, so the target is a single $e^{-st}$ factor.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Copy the integrand and label $e^{-3t}$ as the real damping factor and $e^{-j\\omega t}$ as the oscillation factor. The exponent form is now ready for one substitution.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(\\sigma,\\omega)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}x(t)e^{-3t}e^{-j\\omega t}\\,dt=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}x(t)e^{-st}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use $e^{-\\sigma t}e^{-j\\omega t}=e^{-st}$ from the previous steps with $\\sigma=3$, so the integral can be rewritten directly in Laplace form.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "s=\\sigma+j\\omega=3+j\\omega",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Final result: the rewritten expression is $X(s)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}x(t)e^{-st}\\,dt$, with $\\sigma=3$ as the real part of $s$ and unchanged angular part from $\\omega$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "The Complex Variable $s=\\sigma+j\\omega$",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Interpreting the Weighting and Frequency Roles of a Given $s$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $s=-1+j4$, explain what the real and imaginary parts imply about the weighting and frequency of $e^{-st}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the cue $e^{-st}=e^{-\\sigma t}e^{-j\\omega t}$, and read the given value as a concrete decomposition target.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Separate $s$ into parts: in $s=-1+j4$, the real part is $\\sigma=-1$ and the imaginary coefficient is $\\omega=4$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "s=-1+j4 \\Rightarrow \\sigma=-1,\\;\\omega=4",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply the sign rule to $e^{-\\sigma t}$. Because $\\sigma=-1$, the real part gives $e^{-(-1)t}=e^{t}$, which is growing in time; if $\\sigma>0$, that same term would decay.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{-st}=e^{-(-1)t}e^{-j4t}=e^{t}e^{-j4t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the imaginary part for oscillation: $\\omega=4$ gives sinusoidal frequency $4$ in the factor $e^{-j4t}$, so the full interpretation is growth modulation with angular frequency $4$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
